{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RECOMMENDED FIX, grounded in the file's own existing patterns.\n\nPRIMARY: make jira_dc_pat SESSION-scoped. Every consumer needs only *a* valid PAT — no test asserts\nper-token behaviour — so one token per run replaces one per test, turning consumption from O(tests)\ninto O(1). That is the fix that actually scales, and it matters now because J9\n[rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36] is about to add more live tests to this tier. If a future test genuinely\nneeds a distinct token (revocation, expiry), it gets its own fixture.\n\nCOMPLEMENTARY: add a session-start sweep of leftover 'rebar-j5-harness-*' tokens, mirroring\n_leaked_scratch_projects() at conftest.py:181, which already does exactly this for scratch PROJECTS\nleft by interrupted runs ('any RBJ project still present at session start is residue from a run whose\nteardown did not finish — exactly the stale state that must not silently leak forward'). The same\nreasoning applies verbatim to tokens; without it a crashed run poisons the next.\n\nNOT ASSERTED, needs live confirmation: per-test teardown via DELETE /rest/pat/latest/tokens/{id} would\nalso work, but the fixture only asserts 'rawToken' and I have NOT verified the creation response\ncarries an 'id'. Check before designing around it.\n\nCONTEXT THAT EXPLAINS THE INTERMITTENCY: the fixture mints with expirationDuration=1, so tokens\nself-clear after a day. The cap is therefore not permanent — which is why some runs pass and others\nhit it. That makes this look flaky when it is actually deterministic in the number of PAT-consuming\ntests per session."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785494630695580001,"uuid":"376277c1-97c4-414f-80c8-a778fee2871e"}